('animal-21','animal','young','easy','Which mammal lays eggs?','["Platypus","Kangaroo","Dolphin","Bat"]'::jsonb,0,'The platypus is a mammal that lays eggs.','',''),
('animal-22','animal','young','easy','What do we call a group of lions?','["A pride","A pack","A herd","A flock"]'::jsonb,0,'A group of lions is called a pride.','',''),
('animal-23','animal','young','easy','Which big cat has a mane?','["Male lion","Tiger","Leopard","Cheetah"]'::jsonb,0,'Adult male lions usually grow manes.','',''),
('animal-24','animal','young','easy','What is the fastest land animal?','["Cheetah","Lion","Horse","Antelope"]'::jsonb,0,'The cheetah is the fastest land animal.','',''),
('animal-25','animal','young','easy','Which animal is famous for building dams?','["Beaver","Otter","Muskrat","Duck"]'::jsonb,0,'Beavers build dams from branches, mud and other materials.','',''),
('animal-26','animal','old','medium','What do we call an animal that eats both plants and animals?','["Omnivore","Herbivore","Carnivore","Scavenger"]'::jsonb,0,'Omnivores eat both plant and animal foods.','',''),
('animal-27','animal','old','medium','A caterpillar can change into which insect?','["Butterfly","Bee","Beetle","Ant"]'::jsonb,0,'Many caterpillars transform into butterflies.','',''),
('animal-28','animal','old','medium','What is the largest land animal?','["African elephant","Rhino","Hippo","Giraffe"]'::jsonb,0,'The African elephant is the largest living land animal.','',''),
('animal-29','animal','old','medium','What is a young kangaroo called?','["Joey","Cub","Kit","Fawn"]'::jsonb,0,'A young kangaroo is called a joey.','',''),
('animal-30','animal','old','medium','Which animal is known for a very long neck?','["Giraffe","Zebra","Buffalo","Hyena"]'::jsonb,0,'A giraffe’s long neck helps it reach high leaves.','',''),
('animal-31','animal','old','medium','Which animal is famous for storing food in its cheeks?','["Hamster","Horse","Lion","Giraffe"]'::jsonb,0,'Hamsters can carry food in expandable cheek pouches.','',''),
('animal-32','animal','old','medium','Which animal is the largest living bird?','["Ostrich","Eagle","Penguin","Flamingo"]'::jsonb,0,'The ostrich is the largest living bird.','',''),
('animal-33','animal','old','medium','What is a baby goat called?','["Kid","Calf","Foal","Lamb"]'::jsonb,0,'A young goat is called a kid.','',''),
('animal-34','animal','old','medium','What is a baby horse called?','["Foal","Puppy","Calf","Chick"]'::jsonb,0,'A young horse is called a foal.','',''),
('animal-35','animal','old','medium','Which animal is famous for its black-and-white colouring and lives in Antarctica?','["Penguin","Camel","Lion","Gorilla"]'::jsonb,0,'Penguins are flightless birds found in the Southern Hemisphere, especially Antarctica.','',''),
('animal-36','animal','young','medium','Which animal has a shell and can move very slowly?','["Tortoise","Cheetah","Eagle","Dolphin"]'::jsonb,0,'Tortoises have protective shells and generally move slowly.','',''),
('animal-37','animal','young','medium','What is a group of fish called?','["School","Pride","Flock","Herd"]'::jsonb,0,'A group of fish swimming together is called a school.','',''),
('animal-38','animal','young','medium','Which animal is known for laughing-like calls?','["Hyena","Giraffe","Zebra","Rhino"]'::jsonb,0,'Some hyena calls sound like laughter.','',''),
('animal-8x1','animal','young','medium','Why do mammals such as humans breathe with lungs instead of gills?','["Lungs exchange gases from air","Lungs digest food","Lungs pump blood","Lungs make bones"]'::jsonb,0,'Lungs take oxygen from air and remove carbon dioxide.','',''),
('animal-8x2','animal','young','medium','A food chain starts with grass. Which organism is the primary consumer?','["Grasshopper","Hawk","Snake","Fungus"]'::jsonb,0,'A primary consumer eats producers such as grass.','',''),
('animal-8x3','animal','young','medium','Why are polar bears mostly white?','["Camouflage in snowy habitats","To stay warm","To attract fish","Because their bones are white"]'::jsonb,0,'White fur helps polar bears blend into snowy surroundings.','',''),
('animal-8x4','animal','young','medium','Which adaptation helps a camel survive long periods with little water?','["It can conserve water efficiently","It stores water in its hump","It never loses body heat","It drinks only salt water"]'::jsonb,0,'Camels have adaptations that reduce water loss; the hump stores fat, not water.','',''),
('animal-8x5','animal','young','medium','What is the main role of a predator in an ecosystem?','["It hunts other organisms for food","It makes sunlight","It produces oxygen from rocks","It turns into a plant"]'::jsonb,0,'Predators help control populations by feeding on prey.','',''),
('animal-8x6','animal','young','medium','Which statement best describes metamorphosis?','["A major change in body form during development","An animal learning to hunt","An animal changing its habitat","A plant making seeds"]'::jsonb,0,'Metamorphosis is a major change in body form during development, as in frogs and butterflies.','',''),
('animal-8x7','animal','young','medium','Why do birds have hollow or lightweight bones?','["They help reduce body weight for flight","They store extra water","They make birds warmer","They produce feathers"]'::jsonb,0,'Many flying birds have lightweight skeletons that help with flight.','',''),
('animal-8x8','animal','young','medium','Which animal relationship is an example of mutualism?','["Bees get nectar while flowers are pollinated","A lion eats a zebra","A tick feeds on a dog","A hawk catches a mouse"]'::jsonb,0,'Both bees and flowering plants benefit from the interaction.','',''),
('animal-8x9','animal','young','medium','If a habitat loses most of its plants, what is likely to happen first to herbivores?','["Their food supply decreases","They become carnivores","They stop needing water","Their bodies become larger"]'::jsonb,0,'Herbivores depend directly on plants for food.','',''),
('animal-8x10','animal','young','medium','Why do some animals migrate seasonally?','["To find food, breeding grounds, or better conditions","To change their species","To avoid ever sleeping","To grow new bones"]'::jsonb,0,'Migration can help animals find food, reproduce, or survive seasonal changes.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-medium-1','animal','old','medium','A population of rabbits suddenly has many more foxes hunting them. Which change is most likely first?','["The rabbit population decreases","The grass disappears immediately","The foxes stop needing food","All rabbits become predators"]'::jsonb,0,'More predators usually increase prey deaths.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-medium-2','animal','old','medium','Why does a camel’s hump help it survive in a desert?','["It stores fat that can be used for energy","It stores a large tank of water","It makes the camel breathe underwater","It keeps the camel from sweating"]'::jsonb,0,'A hump stores fat, which can be used for energy when food is scarce.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-medium-3','animal','old','medium','A bird has a long, narrow beak that can reach deep into flowers. What is this most likely an adaptation for?','["Getting nectar from flowers","Crushing large bones","Digging deep burrows","Catching fish underwater"]'::jsonb,0,'A long narrow beak can help a bird reach nectar that other animals cannot easily reach.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-medium-4','animal','old','medium','Two species both eat the same seeds in one habitat. What relationship are they most likely experiencing?','["Competition","Mutualism","Parasitism","Decomposition"]'::jsonb,0,'When organisms depend on the same limited resource, they compete.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-medium-5','animal','old','medium','Why would an animal with thick fur generally struggle more in a very hot environment?','["It may have difficulty releasing excess body heat","Its bones become weaker","It cannot find any food","Its eyes stop working"]'::jsonb,0,'Thick insulation can make heat loss harder in hot conditions.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-1','animal','old','difficult','A grassland loses 80% of its grasses after a long drought. Which sequence is most likely?','["Herbivores decline, then predators may decline","Predators increase, then grasses increase immediately","Carnivores become producers","Decomposers stop working"]'::jsonb,0,'Less plant food can reduce herbivores, which can then reduce food available to predators.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-2','animal','old','difficult','In a food web, insects eat plants, frogs eat insects, and snakes eat frogs. If pesticides greatly reduce insects, what is the most likely short-term effect?','["Frogs may decrease because less food is available","Frogs immediately increase because there are fewer insects","Plants must decrease because frogs eat plants","Snakes immediately increase because frogs have more food"]'::jsonb,0,'Removing a major prey source can cause the next consumer level to decline.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-3','animal','old','difficult','A biologist finds an animal that is warm-blooded, has hair, produces milk, and lays eggs. Which conclusion is best?','["It is a monotreme mammal","It is a reptile","It is an amphibian","It is a bird"]'::jsonb,0,'Monotremes are unusual mammals that lay eggs.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-4','animal','old','difficult','Why can a small change in one species sometimes affect many other species in a food web?','["Species are connected through feeding relationships","Every species eats exactly the same food","Food webs contain only predators","Plants cannot survive without predators"]'::jsonb,0,'A food web contains many linked feeding relationships, so a change can spread through several levels.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-5','animal','old','difficult','A population of moths contains mostly pale moths. After tree bark becomes dark from pollution, dark moths survive more often. What does this illustrate?','["Natural selection","Photosynthesis","Digestion","Migration"]'::jsonb,0,'If inherited traits affect survival and reproduction, natural selection can change population frequencies.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-6','animal','old','difficult','Which observation would provide the strongest evidence that two animal species are competing for the same limited resource?','["When the resource becomes scarce, both populations show reduced access to it","Both species have the same colour","Both species sleep during the day","Both species are mammals"]'::jsonb,0,'Direct evidence of shared use of a limited resource is stronger than similarities in appearance.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-7','animal','old','difficult','A parasite population increases inside a host population. Which outcome could occur if the parasite causes serious disease?','["Host survival or reproduction may decrease","Hosts become producers","Parasites begin photosynthesis","The habitat instantly disappears"]'::jsonb,0,'A harmful parasite can reduce host health, survival, or reproduction.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-8','animal','old','difficult','Why are genetic differences within a species important when the environment changes?','["Some individuals may have traits that improve survival","All individuals will automatically change their genes","Differences prevent reproduction","Genetic variation makes food unnecessary"]'::jsonb,0,'Variation gives natural selection differences to act upon.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-9','animal','old','difficult','A frog population is declining because a wetland is drying. Which evidence would best test whether habitat loss is responsible?','["Compare frog populations in similar wetlands with different water availability","Count only the number of frog colours","Measure the length of the frogs once","Observe whether frogs can jump"]'::jsonb,0,'A comparison with similar habitats helps isolate the relationship between water availability and population size.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-10','animal','old','difficult','Why can removing a top predator sometimes cause an increase in plant damage?','["Its prey may increase and consume more herbivores or other species that affect plants","Predators directly produce plant food","Plants need predators to make sunlight","Removing predators makes soil instantly fertile"]'::jsonb,0,'Predator removal can trigger a trophic cascade through the food web.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-11','animal','old','difficult','Which combination best describes an adaptation?','["A heritable trait that can improve survival or reproduction in a particular environment","A temporary injury","A behaviour learned once that changes DNA","Any feature that looks unusual"]'::jsonb,0,'Adaptations are inherited characteristics shaped over generations by selection.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-12','animal','old','difficult','A scientist observes that a population of finches with deeper beaks survives a drought better because the remaining seeds are hard. What prediction follows?','["If the pattern continues, deeper beaks may become more common over generations","Every finch will grow a deeper beak during the drought","Beak depth cannot vary","The seeds will become softer because of the birds"]'::jsonb,0,'Differential survival and reproduction can change trait frequencies over generations.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-13','animal','old','difficult','Why are decomposers essential to a stable ecosystem?','["They return nutrients from dead material to forms other organisms can use","They create sunlight","They stop all predation","They prevent plants from reproducing"]'::jsonb,0,'Decomposers recycle nutrients from dead organisms and waste.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-14','animal','old','difficult','An animal is active at night, has large eyes, and has highly sensitive hearing. What is the best inference?','["These traits may help it find food or avoid danger in low light","The animal must live underwater","The animal cannot communicate","The traits prove it is a reptile"]'::jsonb,0,'Large eyes and sensitive hearing can be useful for nocturnal activity.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-15','animal','old','difficult','Why might a species with a very narrow diet be more vulnerable to habitat change than an omnivore?','["It may lose its main food source more easily","It can always eat any plant or animal","It does not need a habitat","Omnivores cannot adapt"]'::jsonb,0,'Specialists may depend heavily on particular resources.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-16','animal','old','difficult','A disease kills many individuals of one prey species. Which predator response is most plausible if it depends heavily on that prey?','["Its population may later decline because food is scarcer","Its population must immediately double","It will become a producer","It will no longer need energy"]'::jsonb,0,'Predators that rely strongly on a declining prey species can face food shortages.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-17','animal','old','difficult','Which evidence would best distinguish camouflage from warning coloration?','["Camouflage makes an animal harder to detect, while warning coloration makes it conspicuous to signal danger or unpalatability","Both always make animals invisible","Warning coloration only occurs in mammals","Camouflage always means the animal is poisonous"]'::jsonb,0,'The two strategies have opposite effects on visibility and communicate different survival advantages.','',''),
('animal-kingdom-8-difficult-18','animal','old','difficult','Why can island animals evolve differently from closely related mainland animals?','["Isolation can reduce gene flow while different environments create different selection pressures","Islands have no natural selection","Mainland animals cannot reproduce","All island animals have identical genes"]'::jsonb,0,'Geographic isolation can allow populations to diverge over time.','',''),
