('space-8x7','space','young','medium','Why does the Sun appear brighter than other stars?','["It is much closer to Earth","It is the largest star in the universe","It is the only star with light","It is made of solid rock"]'::jsonb,0,'The Sun is our nearest star, so it appears much brighter than distant stars.','',''),
('space-8x8','space','young','medium','What is an orbit?','["The path one object follows around another object","A planet’s atmosphere","A star’s surface","A crater on the Moon"]'::jsonb,0,'An orbit is the path followed by an object under gravity around another object.','',''),
('space-8x9','space','young','medium','Which force keeps planets in orbit around the Sun?','["Gravity","Sound","Magnetism from Earth","Friction"]'::jsonb,0,'The Sun’s gravity keeps planets moving in their orbits.','',''),
('space-8x10','space','young','medium','Why do we have seasons on Earth?','["Earth’s tilted axis changes how sunlight is received during its orbit","Earth moves much closer to the Sun every summer","The Moon blocks sunlight in winter","The Sun changes size each season"]'::jsonb,0,'Earth’s axial tilt causes different parts of Earth to receive sunlight at different angles during the year.','',''),
('space-station-8-medium-1','space','old','medium','If Earth suddenly had no atmosphere, which change would be most immediate?','["There would be no breathable air at the surface","Earth would stop orbiting the Sun","The Moon would disappear","Gravity would become zero"]'::jsonb,0,'The atmosphere supplies the gases needed for breathing and affects many surface processes.','',''),
('space-station-8-medium-2','space','old','medium','Why does the Moon have much more obvious craters than Earth?','["Earth has erosion and active geology that erase many old craters","The Moon has no rocks","Earth is closer to the Sun","The Moon has stronger winds"]'::jsonb,0,'Weathering, erosion, oceans and geology continually modify Earth’s surface.','',''),
('space-station-8-medium-3','space','old','medium','A planet takes 400 Earth days to orbit its star. What does that tell you?','["Its year is about 400 Earth days long","Its day must also be 400 days","It has no gravity","It must be larger than Earth"]'::jsonb,0,'A year is the time required to complete one orbit.','',''),
('space-station-8-medium-4','space','old','medium','Why can we see stars that are no longer in the same state they are today?','["Their light takes time to travel to Earth","Stars stop producing light at night","Telescopes create old stars","Gravity reverses time"]'::jsonb,0,'We observe distant objects through light that may have travelled for years or much longer.','',''),
('space-station-8-medium-5','space','old','medium','If a planet has no natural satellites, which statement is safest?','["It simply means no moon has been detected orbiting it","It cannot have gravity","It cannot rotate","It cannot orbit a star"]'::jsonb,0,'A planet can exist without a natural satellite.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-1','space','old','difficult','Why does observing a distant galaxy allow astronomers to study its past?','["Its light needs time to travel across space before reaching us","Galaxies send memories through gravity","Telescopes move backward in time","Stars only exist after we observe them"]'::jsonb,0,'The farther away an object is, the longer its light has taken to reach us.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-2','space','old','difficult','A star’s spectrum contains dark lines at specific wavelengths. What can astronomers infer from these lines?','["The lines can reveal which elements are present","The star must be a planet","The lines prove the star has no gravity","The spectrum tells only its age"]'::jsonb,0,'Atoms absorb or emit characteristic wavelengths, creating spectral signatures.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-3','space','old','difficult','Why does an astronaut in orbit feel weightless even though Earth’s gravity is still acting strongly?','["The astronaut and spacecraft are continuously falling around Earth together","Gravity disappears above the atmosphere","There is no mass in space","The spacecraft is outside Earth’s gravitational field"]'::jsonb,0,'Orbital motion is continuous free fall around Earth.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-4','space','old','difficult','A planet orbits closer to its star than Earth does. Which is generally expected if the star is similar to the Sun?','["The planet completes an orbit in less time","The planet must have no atmosphere","Its gravity must be zero","It cannot have seasons"]'::jsonb,0,'Closer planets generally have shorter orbital periods under the same general gravitational conditions.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-5','space','old','difficult','What would happen to the apparent position of a nearby star if Earth moves to the opposite side of its orbit?','["Its position can appear to shift slightly against much more distant stars","The star would physically move instantly","The Sun would stop shining","The star would become a planet"]'::jsonb,0,'This apparent shift is called stellar parallax.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-6','space','old','difficult','Why are radio telescopes useful even though humans cannot see radio waves?','["They detect wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation outside visible light","They turn radio waves into gravity","They only work on the Moon","Radio waves are sound waves"]'::jsonb,0,'Radio telescopes observe electromagnetic radiation at radio wavelengths.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-7','space','old','difficult','A black hole is difficult to see directly mainly because','["Light cannot escape from inside its event horizon","It contains no mass","It is made only of empty space","It reflects all light perfectly"]'::jsonb,0,'No light escapes from within the event horizon, so the black hole itself is not directly visible.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-8','space','old','difficult','If the Sun were replaced by a star with the same mass but much less brightness, what would happen to Earth’s orbit immediately?','["Its orbit would be nearly unchanged by brightness alone","Earth would instantly stop orbiting","Earth would become weightless","The Moon would leave the Solar System"]'::jsonb,0,'Orbital motion depends primarily on mass and distance, not visible brightness.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-9','space','old','difficult','Why does a total lunar eclipse occur only when the Sun, Earth and Moon are suitably aligned?','["Earth must pass between the Sun and Moon so Earth’s shadow falls on the Moon","The Moon must pass between Earth and the Sun","The Sun must stop producing light","The Moon creates Earth’s shadow"]'::jsonb,0,'A lunar eclipse requires the Moon to enter Earth’s shadow.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-10','space','old','difficult','What is the strongest reason seasons are not caused simply by Earth being closer to the Sun in summer?','["Earth’s axial tilt changes the angle and duration of sunlight received by each hemisphere","The Sun changes size every season","Earth stops rotating in winter","The Moon controls Earth’s temperature"]'::jsonb,0,'Axial tilt changes solar angle and daylight duration, producing seasons.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-11','space','old','difficult','A spacecraft sends a signal to Earth from a much greater distance. What unavoidable effect increases?','["The communication delay","The spacecraft’s mass","The speed of light","Earth’s gravity"]'::jsonb,0,'Signals travel at finite speed, so greater distance means longer communication time.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-12','space','old','difficult','Why can a planet with a thick atmosphere have a much hotter surface than expected from its distance from the Sun?','["Atmospheric gases can produce a strong greenhouse effect","Thick atmospheres always reflect all sunlight","Gravity creates sunlight","Oceans create stars"]'::jsonb,0,'Greenhouse gases can trap outgoing infrared energy and raise surface temperature.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-13','space','old','difficult','If two stars have the same surface temperature but one appears brighter from Earth, what could explain this?','["The brighter-looking star could be much closer or intrinsically more luminous","Temperature alone determines apparent brightness","The dimmer star must have no gravity","The brighter star must be a planet"]'::jsonb,0,'Apparent brightness depends on intrinsic luminosity and distance.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-14','space','old','difficult','What observation would most strongly support the idea that an exoplanet has an atmosphere?','["Spectral features of gases appear in the planet’s transmitted or reflected light","The planet has a circular orbit","The planet has a name","The planet is farther away than Earth"]'::jsonb,0,'Atmospheric gases can leave identifiable spectral signatures.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-15','space','old','difficult','Why is the cosmic microwave background important to cosmology?','["It is relic radiation from the early universe and carries information about its early state","It is light from Earth’s atmosphere","It is produced by today’s thunderstorms","It proves every star is the same age"]'::jsonb,0,'The CMB is leftover radiation from the early hot universe.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-16','space','old','difficult','If a star runs out of hydrogen fuel in its core, what can happen next?','["Its core and surrounding layers can change as new fusion stages or collapse processes begin","The star immediately becomes a planet","All gravity disappears","Its light travels backward"]'::jsonb,0,'Changes in core fuel can alter the balance between gravity and pressure and lead to later stellar stages.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-17','space','old','difficult','Why can a planet remain in orbit instead of falling straight into its star?','["Its forward motion and the star’s gravity combine to create a curved orbital path","Gravity pushes the planet away","The planet has no mass","Space contains invisible walls"]'::jsonb,0,'Orbital velocity gives the planet sideways motion while gravity continually curves its path.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-18','space','old','difficult','Which discovery would be strongest evidence of liquid water having existed on Mars in the past?','["Mineral deposits and landforms formed by long-lasting water activity","A picture of Mars looking red","Mars having two moons","Mars being farther from the Sun than Earth"]'::jsonb,0,'Certain minerals and geological structures can preserve evidence of ancient water.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-19','space','old','difficult','Why do astronomers use different kinds of telescopes, such as infrared and X-ray telescopes?','["Different wavelengths reveal different physical processes and objects","All wavelengths carry exactly the same information","Visible light cannot travel through space","X-rays are easier for human eyes to see"]'::jsonb,0,'Different wavelengths reveal different temperatures, materials and energetic processes.','',''),
('space-station-8-difficult-20','space','old','difficult','A galaxy’s spectral lines are shifted toward longer wavelengths. What can this indicate?','["The galaxy is moving away from us relative to the observer","The galaxy has stopped producing light","The galaxy has no stars","Its gravity has become zero"]'::jsonb,0,'A redshift can indicate recession due to relative motion and, on cosmic scales, expansion.','',''),
