('ocean-8x1','ocean','young','medium','Why is most deep ocean water very cold?','["Sunlight warms mainly the surface, while deep water receives little direct solar energy","Deep water is closer to the Sun","Fish cool the water","Salt creates ice"]'::jsonb,0,'Sunlight penetrates only the upper ocean, so deep water remains much colder.','',''),
('ocean-8x2','ocean','young','medium','What is the main source of energy for most ocean surface ecosystems?','["Sunlight","Moonlight","Earth’s core","Ocean waves"]'::jsonb,0,'Phytoplankton use sunlight to make food, supporting many marine food webs.','',''),
('ocean-8x3','ocean','young','medium','Why are coral reefs often found in warm, shallow seas?','["Their coral-building organisms need suitable light and water conditions","They need complete darkness","They grow only in freshwater","They feed on sand"]'::jsonb,0,'Many reef-building corals depend on symbiotic algae that require light.','',''),
('ocean-8x4','ocean','young','medium','What is an estuary?','["A place where freshwater meets and mixes with seawater","A deep ocean trench","A frozen lake","A coral skeleton"]'::jsonb,0,'Estuaries form where rivers or streams meet the sea and their waters mix.','',''),
('ocean-8x5','ocean','young','medium','Why are mangrove forests important to coastal areas?','["They provide habitat and help protect shorelines","They make oceans freshwater","They prevent all storms","They remove every fish from shallow water"]'::jsonb,0,'Mangroves shelter wildlife and can reduce erosion and wave energy.','',''),
('ocean-8x6','ocean','young','medium','What causes ocean tides mainly?','["The gravitational pull of the Moon and, to a lesser extent, the Sun","Underwater volcanoes","Fish movement","Ocean temperature alone"]'::jsonb,0,'Tides are driven mainly by lunar gravity, with solar gravity also contributing.','',''),
('ocean-8x7','ocean','young','medium','Why can a whale stay underwater for a long time despite breathing air?','["It stores oxygen efficiently and slows its metabolism during dives","It breathes through its skin","It has gills","It never needs oxygen"]'::jsonb,0,'Whales store oxygen in blood and muscles and can reduce activity during dives.','',''),
('ocean-8x8','ocean','young','medium','Which ocean zone receives enough sunlight for photosynthesis?','["Photic zone","Abyssal zone","Hadal zone","Midnight zone"]'::jsonb,0,'The photic zone is the sunlit upper layer where photosynthesis can occur.','',''),
('ocean-8x9','ocean','young','medium','Why does salt water generally have a higher density than fresh water?','["Dissolved salts add mass without greatly increasing volume","Salt removes all water","Salt makes water warmer","Salt creates bubbles"]'::jsonb,0,'Dissolved salts increase the mass of a given volume of water.','',''),
('ocean-8x10','ocean','young','medium','What role do decomposers play in marine ecosystems?','["They break down dead material and recycle nutrients","They create sunlight","They stop tides","They turn fish into rocks"]'::jsonb,0,'Decomposers help return nutrients from dead organisms to the ecosystem.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-medium-1','ocean','old','medium','Why is dissolved oxygen important to many fish?','["They use it for cellular respiration","They use it to make sunlight","It replaces all food","It makes seawater fresh"]'::jsonb,0,'Many aquatic animals need dissolved oxygen for respiration.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-medium-2','ocean','old','medium','Why can a coral reef be harmed when seawater becomes unusually warm for too long?','["Corals can lose their symbiotic algae and bleach","Corals turn into fish","Warm water removes all salt","The reef becomes a freshwater lake"]'::jsonb,0,'Heat stress can cause corals to expel their symbiotic algae.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-medium-3','ocean','old','medium','Why does a deep-sea animal experience enormous pressure?','["The weight of the water above it is very large","The animal creates pressure","Sunlight increases pressure","Deep water has no mass"]'::jsonb,0,'Pressure increases with depth because of the weight of water above.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-medium-4','ocean','old','medium','Why is upwelling often linked with productive fisheries?','["It can bring nutrient-rich deep water toward the surface","It removes all nutrients from the ocean","It warms every deep-water species","It stops plankton from growing"]'::jsonb,0,'Nutrients brought upward can support phytoplankton and larger food webs.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-medium-5','ocean','old','medium','Why do some fish have streamlined bodies?','["A streamlined shape can reduce resistance while swimming","It makes them heavier","It prevents them from using oxygen","It lets them live without water"]'::jsonb,0,'Streamlining can reduce drag in water.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-1','ocean','old','difficult','A coastal region experiences strong seasonal upwelling. Why might fish catches increase there?','["Nutrients rise into sunlit water, supporting phytoplankton and a larger food web","Deep water contains no nutrients","Upwelling removes all oxygen everywhere","Fish are pushed upward by sunlight"]'::jsonb,0,'Nutrient-rich water can stimulate primary production and support more consumers.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-2','ocean','old','difficult','Why can melting a large glacier on land raise sea level more directly than melting an equal volume of floating sea ice?','["Land ice adds new water to the ocean, while floating ice already displaces water","Floating ice contains no water","Land ice is always saltier","Sea ice is outside Earth’s gravity"]'::jsonb,0,'Adding land-based meltwater increases ocean volume; floating ice already displaces water.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-3','ocean','old','difficult','If phytoplankton populations suddenly collapsed across a large ocean region, which effect would be most concerning?','["Reduced food supply for organisms that depend on them and reduced carbon uptake","More oxygen production everywhere","Immediate growth of all fish populations","The ocean would stop having tides"]'::jsonb,0,'Phytoplankton are major primary producers and important in carbon cycling.','','')
insert into public.quizy_questions (id,land_id,age_band,difficulty,question,options,answer_index,explanation,word,hint) values
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-4','ocean','old','difficult','Why does sound generally travel faster in seawater than in air?','["Water’s physical properties allow pressure disturbances to travel faster through it","Water has no particles","Sound travels only through sunlight","Air has no molecules"]'::jsonb,0,'Sound speed depends on the medium’s elasticity and density; it is faster in water than air.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-5','ocean','old','difficult','A marine food web loses most of its large predatory fish. What is a possible trophic cascade?','["Some prey populations may rise and then alter lower levels of the food web","All species immediately become producers","Phytoplankton stop photosynthesising","Tides become weaker"]'::jsonb,0,'Removing predators can allow prey to increase, producing cascading effects.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-6','ocean','old','difficult','Why are hydrothermal vents able to support ecosystems without sunlight?','["Chemosynthetic microbes use chemical energy from vent fluids","Sunlight is stronger at the seafloor","Fish produce sunlight","Vents contain no bacteria"]'::jsonb,0,'Some microbes obtain energy by oxidising chemicals rather than using sunlight.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-7','ocean','old','difficult','What is the thermocline?','["A layer where water temperature changes rapidly with depth","A deep-sea animal","A type of coral","The deepest ocean trench"]'::jsonb,0,'The thermocline is a zone of rapid temperature change with depth.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-8','ocean','old','difficult','Why can excess fertiliser runoff cause fish kills in coastal waters?','["Nutrient enrichment can fuel algal blooms whose decay consumes dissolved oxygen","Fertiliser instantly removes all salt","Algae always produce too much oxygen at night","Fish cannot detect nutrients"]'::jsonb,0,'Decomposition of excess organic matter can lower oxygen and create hypoxic conditions.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-9','ocean','old','difficult','Why can mangrove roots reduce damage from waves and storms?','["Dense roots can slow water movement and trap sediment","Roots create hurricanes","Mangroves remove gravity","Their leaves turn waves into freshwater"]'::jsonb,0,'Mangrove root systems can dissipate wave energy and stabilize sediment.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-10','ocean','old','difficult','Why is the open ocean often less productive per unit area than a nutrient-rich coastal upwelling zone?','["Deep nutrients may be far from the sunlit surface where phytoplankton can use them","The open ocean has no sunlight","Coastal water has no organisms","Ocean currents stop everywhere offshore"]'::jsonb,0,'Nutrients and light availability strongly influence marine primary production.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-11','ocean','old','difficult','If ocean acidity increases, why can organisms that build calcium carbonate shells or skeletons be affected?','["More acidic conditions can make carbonate chemistry less favourable for calcification","Acidification makes all shells instantly stronger","Shells are made from sunlight","Acidity changes gravity"]'::jsonb,0,'Ocean acidification alters carbonate chemistry and can make calcification harder for some organisms.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-12','ocean','old','difficult','Why does a whale need to surface even though it lives in the ocean?','["It is a mammal and must breathe atmospheric oxygen with lungs","Whales have gills that stop working underwater","Whales cannot store oxygen","The ocean contains no oxygen"]'::jsonb,0,'Whales breathe air with lungs.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-13','ocean','old','difficult','What makes a keystone species especially important?','["Its effect on an ecosystem can be much larger than expected from its abundance","It is always the largest animal","It must live on land","It never interacts with other species"]'::jsonb,0,'Keystone species can have disproportionately large ecological effects.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-14','ocean','old','difficult','Why can a dead zone form after nutrient pollution enters a coastal ecosystem?','["Excess nutrients can increase organic matter whose decomposition consumes oxygen","Nutrients freeze the ocean","Fish consume all oxygen directly in minutes","Salt disappears"]'::jsonb,0,'Decomposition of excess biomass can consume dissolved oxygen.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-15','ocean','old','difficult','Why are deep-ocean currents important to Earth’s climate system?','["They redistribute heat and dissolved substances around the global ocean","They stop evaporation everywhere","They only move during storms","They create sunlight"]'::jsonb,0,'Large-scale ocean circulation transports heat and materials.','',''),
('ocean-deep-8-difficult-16','ocean','old','difficult','Why can plastic pollution harm marine animals even when the plastic is not poisonous?','["Animals may become entangled or ingest pieces that block or damage their digestive systems","Plastic always turns into oxygen","Plastic makes fish photosynthesise","Animals cannot interact with floating objects"]'::jsonb,0,'Physical entanglement and ingestion are major hazards of marine debris.','',''),
